# Monthly updates: dedicated facts, one operation

## Usage (caller's view)

The browser opens `/updates`; SvelteKit's server load calls Rust `/updates`, validates its response, and passes `Updates` to a page showing MPs and Companies. Every entity has its existing `/members/{id}` or `/funders/{canonical_id}` link. The result is complete, without a top-ten cutoff or monetary fields.

```rust
// HTTP shell owns the request clock; service owns what current month means.
let updates = state.updates_service.updates(Utc::now()).await?;
// Production construction follows rankings.
let updates_service = Arc::new(updates::Service::new(db.clone()));
// Deterministic service test controls time, without a clock abstraction.
let result = service.updates("2026-10-26T12:00:00Z".parse()?).await?;
```

```ts
// routes/updates/+page.server.ts, following rankings' origin/error handling.
return { updates: await loadUpdates(fetch, request.signal, origin), error: null };
// Feature derives existing-profile links; shared presentation does not know routes.
const href = `/funders/${entry.id}`;
```

## Problem and grounding

Current rankings use `RankingsService::rankings()` → `RankingsRepo::ranking_declarations(start)` → one joined SQLx snapshot, then `summarize`. Member rankings include every category but company rankings deliberately restrict to DonationSupport, calculate GBP totals, sort by value, and truncate to ten. The outbound adapter canonicalizes through `funder_alias_groups`, uses `funder_group_names.preferred_name`, parses the root funder's kind, and retains entry rows. Its lower-bound-only query and financial payload do not match monthly updates. `AppState<E,D,R>`, handlers, routes, and server constructors thread the ranking service explicitly. Frontend parsing likewise rejects more than ten and requires financial totals. Reusing those result/transport types would hide semantic errors rather than save useful structure.

## Shape

Choose a dedicated monthly-updates domain/service/repository/handler. Reuse established canonical-resolution SQL joins, error conventions, and server load structure, rather than rankings' financial models. New modules follow `domain/{models,services,repositories}/updates.rs`, `outbound/updates.rs`, `inbound/http/handlers/updates.rs`, frontend `lib/{updates,server/updates}.ts`, and `routes/updates/+page{.server.ts,.svelte}`.

```rust
// models/updates.rs. Fields of the interval remain private.
pub struct RegistrationMonth {
    start: DateTime<Utc>,
    end: DateTime<Utc>,
}
impl RegistrationMonth {
    pub fn containing(now: DateTime<Utc>) -> Self { todo!() }
    pub fn start(&self) -> DateTime<Utc> { todo!() }
    pub fn end(&self) -> DateTime<Utc> { todo!() }
    pub fn includes(&self, registered: DateTime<Utc>) -> bool { todo!() }
}
pub struct UpdatedMember { pub id: MemberId, pub name: String,
    pub party_name: String, pub membership_from: String }
pub struct UpdatedCompany { pub id: FunderId, pub name: String }
pub struct EntityUpdate<I> {
    pub identity: I,
    pub latest_registered_at: DateTime<Utc>,
    pub declaration_count: usize,
}
pub struct Updates {
    pub month: RegistrationMonth,
    pub members: Vec<EntityUpdate<UpdatedMember>>,
    pub companies: Vec<EntityUpdate<UpdatedCompany>>,
}
// Private-to-read-boundary fact shape: no financial/category/party aggregation data.
pub struct UpdateDeclaration {
    pub source_id: i32, // Match the actual persisted declaration ID primitive.
    pub registered_at: DateTime<Utc>,
    pub member: UpdatedMember,
    pub funders: Vec<UpdateFunder>,
}
pub struct UpdateFunder { pub id: FunderId, pub name: String, pub kind: FunderKind }
trait UpdatesRepo {
    fn declarations(&self, month: &RegistrationMonth)
        -> impl Future<Output=Result<Vec<UpdateDeclaration>, UpdatesError>> + Send;
}
trait UpdatesService {
    fn updates(&self, now: DateTime<Utc>)
        -> impl Future<Output=Result<Updates, UpdatesError>> + Send;
}
impl<R: UpdatesRepo> Service<R> {
    // Build month, obtain snapshot, reduce facts, sort; no transport knowledge.
}
impl FunderKind {
    pub const fn is_individual(&self) -> bool { todo!() } // exhaustive match
}
```

`FunderKind::is_individual` owns the classification split. Change rankings to call it too, removing the existing duplicated exhaustive board split. Only `Individual` returns true; all current non-individual variants remain Companies. This preserves existing semantics and ensures future enum additions require a classification decision.

The adapter uses one SQLx macro query with `registration_date >= start AND registration_date < end`. Start from declarations and join members, then LEFT JOIN entries and the existing canonical group/name/root-kind joins. No category predicate. No amount/currency columns. A declaration without entries still yields its MP. Group result rows into declaration facts, parsing stored dates, `MemberId`, `FunderId`, and `FunderKind` at this boundary. Return errors for malformed stored classifications as rankings does, without defaulting arbitrary strings. Existing null-kind behavior stays NotSpecified.

The service reduces into two maps keyed by stable IDs. For each declaration, observe its member once and each canonical non-individual funder once, deduplicating canonical IDs within that declaration. Counts are distinct source declaration IDs, not funding-entry counts; make the accumulator own a set of observed source IDs, or prove the adapter emits one fact per source declaration and deduplicate funders per fact. Prefer an accumulator set to make this invariant local and explicit. Latest date is maximum registration date among that entity's included declarations. Sort each complete vector descending latest date, then ascending stored display name, then ascending stable ID. Use a documented ordinary string ordering, not a new fuzzy normalization rule. Same-name entities remain separate.

`RegistrationMonth` alone owns half-open UTC calendar-month boundaries, including December rollover and leap years. Compute from `now` once per request. Undated declarations are excluded by SQL; next-month and later declarations are excluded. No cap at the request instant: the selected period is the full current month. Do not use update timestamps or change financial totals.

HTTP response owns transport field spellings: `month_start`, `month_end`, `members`, `companies`; each row includes `id`, `name`, `latest_registered_at`, `declaration_count`, with optional member context. Service models retain separate member/funder identity types rather than a stringly entity union. Add `U: UpdatesService` and `updates_service: Arc<U>` to AppState and thread through handler/router bounds and tracing test doubles. This is mechanical integration cost, not a reason to place unrelated behavior in RankingsService.

Frontend models contain no `total` or amount. The server parser validates unknown input, unique IDs per list, valid timestamp boundaries and row dates, safe positive integer counts, and member context. It accepts any list length. Date labels use UTC consistently. Use existing list/row presentation if it supports linked entities without monetary props; otherwise add a narrow reusable row/list in the design system after reading README/COMPOSITION. Do not force an amount-less updates row through `RankingRow`. Page copy stays limited to Updates, current month, MPs, Companies, counts/dates where displayed, and required error/empty text. AppShell gains one Updates navigation entry following the intended existing placement; no explanatory paragraphs.

## Interface depth and red-flag screen

One service call hides date selection, snapshot assembly, canonical grouping, declaration deduplication, and ordering. The repository takes an invariant-bearing interval rather than independently receiving two unrelated timestamps. There is one owner for month policy, company classification, identity resolution (the existing database grouping), and aggregate counting. No shared mutable state, secondary cache, optional financial properties, or caller-coordinated load stages are introduced. The new service adds actual policy rather than forwarding repository output.

## Tradeoffs accepted

- Accept a fourth service parameter through HTTP plumbing in exchange for preserving the meaning of RankingsService.
- Accept a focused joined query resembling rankings' joins in exchange for avoiding loading and parsing unrelated financial data. Extracting a generic read framework would enlarge the interface without useful callers.
- Accept materializing this month's facts in exchange for directly testable pure aggregation and a single database snapshot. If actual data size warrants SQL aggregation, preserve the service contract and change only the adapter/fact boundary.

## Alternatives considered

1. Extend RankingsService with updates(now), and generalize RankingsRepo's start-only query to a typed range. Smaller wiring but monthly all-category activity becomes coupled to financial rankings, reads amounts unnecessarily, and tempts reuse of donation-only filtering. Its public surface hides less coherent knowledge. Rejected.
2. Return SQL-aggregated members/companies directly from a dedicated repository and keep a thin service. Fewer Rust accumulators, but classification/dedup/order rules move into SQL while enum classification remains in Rust. The nominal service becomes a pass-through and finite classification is easy to duplicate as SQL strings. Rejected for this implementation size.
3. Derive the page from existing profiles/rankings in SvelteKit. Cannot reliably discover all entities, lacks an upper bound/canonical-count contract, and recreates business rules on the frontend. Rejected.

## Synthesis decision

Candidate recommendation only; parent synthesizer chooses the final design. Favor dedicated service/facts with a shared FunderKind method. The strongest graft from a reuse candidate would be extracting neutral member/funder identity types only if another concrete caller immediately benefits; do not rename broad existing rankings modules as incidental cleanup.

## Test plan

Pure domain tests: exact start included; prior instant excluded; exact next start excluded; UTC timestamps crossing local midnight; December/year and leap-February boundaries; unknown category admitted; undated absent; all non-individual kinds included; Individual excluded; multiple entries and aliases for one canonical company count once; two companies each count once; multiple declarations aggregate; newest/name/ID ties stable; more than ten survive. Member with no funder survives. No financial fields appear in response.

Adapter integration fixtures: multiple alias IDs → one canonical group/name/root kind; distinct declaration count; empty entries; unknown category; boundary rows and null date. Use SQLx macros and regenerate offline query metadata by repository convention. Existing rankings tests establish classification refactor preserves behavior.

Frontend parser tests and Playwright: unlimited lists, correct existing-profile links, both entity groups, count/date rendering, error/empty responses, no financial display, desktop screenshot after completed implementation. Run required Rust checks plus npm check/build/affected production Playwright tests. Existing financial tests remain unchanged.

## Implementation reconciliation

No implementation performed. No accepted deviations.

## Open questions and risks

Does the desired page prefer both lists visible or a two-choice control? The domain/API does not depend on that choice; use both visible as the minimal default unless the parent has UI direction.

## Next implementation step

Implement RegistrationMonth and pure reduction tests, then the focused SQLx adapter and service integration before the page.
